<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1250 · Egypt &amp; North Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Al-Burda</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ar" dir="rtl">قصيدة البردة</bdi> / <bdi lang="ar-Latn">Qasīdat al-Burda</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A long Arabic poem from 13th-century Egypt in praise of the Prophet Muhammad, recited by Muslims all over the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>al-Busiri</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (recited and chanted)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Muslims, above all Sufi communities</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Egypt</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>13th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by al-Busiri (13th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Qasīdat al-Burda, the &quot;Ode of the Mantle&quot;, is a thirteenth-century ode in praise of Muhammad by al-Busiri, a mystic of the Shadhili <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Its own title is &quot;The Celestial Lights in Praise of the Best of Creation&quot;. The poem's story is told of its author: al-Busiri, afflicted by illness, praised Muhammad without ceasing until the Prophet appeared to him in a dream and wrapped him in a mantle; in the morning he found that God had cured him. The name recalls an older poem, Bānat Suʿād by Ka'b ibn Zuhayr, which was first called al-Burda: Ka'b recited it before Muhammad after embracing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Muhammad, moved, put his own mantle over him. Al-Busiri's poem, in which the mantle comes in a dream, became by far the more famous of the two.</p><p>The Burda has ten chapters and 160 verses, all on one rhyme: every verse ends in the Arabic letter mīm, a form called mīmiyya. A refrain asking God's blessings and peace on the Prophet is sung between the verses. The chapters move from love poetry and warnings against the self's whims to the praise of the Prophet, his birth, his miracles, the Qur'an, his Ascension and his struggles, and end with a plea for his intercession and a personal prayer.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a> have long venerated the poem. It is learned by heart and recited in gatherings, and many Muslims recite it both day to day and on special occasions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawlid">Mawlid</a>, the Prophet's birthday, which makes it one of the most recited poems in the world. Its verses decorate the walls of public buildings and mosques; for centuries they decorated the Prophet's Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, where all but two lines were later erased.</p><p>Mainstream <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> scholars such as Ibn Hajar al-Haytami, Nazifi and Qastallani wrote commentaries on it, over 90 in all, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hajar-al-asqalani">Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani</a> (d. 852 AH) studied it, reading it aloud to his teacher and receiving it in writing from a transmitter who had heard it from al-Busiri himself. Not everyone approved: the Najdi scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> held the poem to be shirk, idolatry. It has been translated into Persian, Turkish, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Berber languages</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, Punjabi, Sindhi, Saraiki, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>, Chinese, French, German, Norwegian and English, among others.</p><p>The Burda also became the model of a kind of poem. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-shawqi">Ahmad Shawqi</a> wrote Nahj al-Burda in its style, and Tamim al-Barghouti's Burdat Tamim, written in November and December 2010 and first published in 2013, is the latest major work in the line; in 2020 he presented it in a five-part video series that first explains the three earlier Burdas, Ka'b's, al-Busiri's and Shawqi's.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A devotional poem of love and praise for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>. It begins with longing and with the poet's regret for his own failings, then recounts the Prophet's birth, miracles, the Qur'an and his night journey, and ends by asking for his intercession and God's mercy. Its mood is yearning, reverent and hopeful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Arabic</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Arabic</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">أمِنْ تذَكُّرِ جيرانٍ بذي سلمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">A-min tadhakkuri jīrānin bi-Dhī Salami</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Is it from remembering neighbours at Dhū Salam</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مزجتَ دمعاً جرى من مقلةٍ بدمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">mazajta damʿan jarā min muqlatin bi-dami</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that you have mixed tears flowing from your eye with blood?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">أمْ هبَّتِ الريحُ من تلقاءِ كاظمةٍ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Am habbati r-rīḥu min tilqāʾi Kāẓimatin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Or has the wind blown from the direction of Kāẓima,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">وأوْمَضَ البَرْقُ في الظلْماءِ مِنْ إضَمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">wa-awmaḍa l-barqu fi ẓ-ẓalmāʾi min Iḍami</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and lightning flashed in the darkness from Iḍam?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">فما لعينيكَ إن قلتَ اكففا هَمَتا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Fa-mā li-ʿaynayka in qulta kfufā hamatā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What ails your eyes, that when you say &quot;hold back&quot; they stream,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">ومَا لِقَلْبِك إنْ قُلْتَ اسْتَفِقْ يَهِمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">wa-mā li-qalbika in qulta stafiq yahimi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and your heart, that when you say &quot;come to your senses&quot; it wanders?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">أَيَحْسَبُ الصَّبُّ أنَّ الحُبَّ مُنْكتِمٌ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">A-yaḥsabu ṣ-ṣabbu anna l-ḥubba munkatimun</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Does the lover think that love can be hidden,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">ما بَيْنَ مُنْسَجِم منهُ ومضطَرِمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">mā bayna munsajimin minhu wa-muḍṭarimi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>between a flood of tears and a heart on fire?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">لولاَ الهَوَى لَمْ تُرِقْ دَمْعاً عَلَى طَلَلٍ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Lawlā l-hawā lam turiq damʿan ʿalā ṭalalin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Were it not for passion you would not shed tears over a ruin,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">ولا أرقتَ لذكرِ البانِ والعَلمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">wa-lā ariqta li-dhikri l-bāni wa-l-ʿalami</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>nor lie sleepless at the mention of the willow and the mountain.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">فكيفَ تُنْكِرُ حُبَّا بعدَ ما شَهِدَتْ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Fa-kayfa tunkiru ḥubban baʿda mā shahidat</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So how can you deny a love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">بهِ عليكَ عدولُ الدَّمْعِ والسَّقَم</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">bihi ʿalayka ʿudūlu d-damʿi wa-s-saqami</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>when the honest witnesses of tears and sickness have testified against you?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">وَأثْبَتَ الوجْدُ خَطَّيْ عَبْرَةٍ وضَنًى</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Wa-athbata l-wajdu khaṭṭay ʿabratin wa-ḍanan</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Longing has fixed two lines of weeping and wasting</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مِثْلَ البَهارِ عَلَى خَدَّيْكَ والعَنَمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">mithla l-bahāri ʿalā khaddayka wa-l-ʿanami</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>on your cheeks, like the yellow of narcissus and the red of the ʿanam berry.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">نعمْ سرى طيفُ من أهوى فأرقني</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Naʿam sarā ṭayfu man ahwā fa-arraqanī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yes, the phantom of the one I love came by night and kept me awake;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">والحُبُّ يَعْتَرِضُ اللَّذاتِ بالأَلَمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">wa-l-ḥubbu yaʿtariḍu l-ladhdhāti bi-l-alami</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>love blocks pleasure with pain.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">يا لائِمِي في الهَوَى العُذْرِيِّ مَعْذِرَةً</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Yā lāʾimī fi l-hawā l-ʿudhriyyi maʿdhiratan</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You who blame me for a chaste love, accept my excuse;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">منِّي إليكَ ولو أنصفتَ لم تلُمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">minnī ilayka wa-law anṣafta lam talumi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>had you been fair, you would not blame.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">عَدَتْكَ حالِيَ لا سِرِّي بمُسْتَتِرٍ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">ʿAdatka ḥāliya lā sirrī bi-mustatirin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May my state pass you by: my secret is not hidden</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">عن الوُشاةِ ولادائي بمنحسمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">ʿani l-wushāti wa-lā dāʾī bi-munḥasimi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>from slanderers, nor is my sickness cut off.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَحَّضَتْنِي النُّصْحَ لكِنْ لَسْتُ أَسْمَعُهُ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Maḥḥaḍatniya n-nuṣḥa lākin lastu asmaʿuhu</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You gave me sincere advice, but I do not hear it;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">إنَّ المُحِبَّ عَن العُذَّالِ في صَمَمِ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">inna l-muḥibba ʿani l-ʿudhdhāli fī ṣamami</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the lover is deaf to those who reproach.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">إني اتهمتُ نصيحَ الشيبِ في عذلٍ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">Innī ttahamtu naṣīḥa sh-shaybi fī ʿadhalin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I even suspected the counsel of grey hair in its reproach,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ar" dir="rtl">والشَّيْبُ أَبْعَدُ في نُصْحٍ عَنِ التُّهَم</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ar-Latn">wa-sh-shaybu abʿadu fī nuṣḥin ʿani t-tuhami</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>though grey hair is the furthest of advisers from suspicion.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: al-Busiri, Qasidat al-Burda (13th century): the first chapter, on love-longing. Text from <a href="https://ar.wikisource.org/wiki/%D8%A3%D9%85%D9%86_%D8%AA%D8%B0%D9%83%D8%B1_%D8%AC%D9%8A%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%A8%D8%B0%D9%8A_%D8%B3%D9%84%D9%85" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ALA-LC romanization of Arabic, with the vowels of the verse).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>The text is the poem's first chapter, the nasīb or love-prelude, one verse to a stanza: twelve of its about 160 verses. The refrain sung between verses in many performances (&quot;Mawlāya ṣalli wa-sallim …&quot;) is a later addition and is not part of this edition.</li><li>Verses 1–2: Dhū Salam, Kāẓima and Iḍam are places on the way to Medina, which is how the love-prelude turns, in the second chapter, into praise of the Prophet.</li><li>Verse 5: the willow (bān) and the mountain (ʿalam) are stock images of the beloved's camp in Arabic love poetry.</li><li>Verse 7: the ʿanam is a shrub with red fruit or blossom; with the yellow narcissus (bahār) it pictures cheeks yellowed by wasting and reddened by bloody tears.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="aOASu5QFG8A" data-title="Al-Burda: A group recitation at the shrine of Nabi Hud (Hadramawt)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">A group recitation at the shrine of Nabi Hud (Hadramawt)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Salat Ala Nabi قناة الصلاة على النبي. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aOASu5QFG8A" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nahj al-Burda (Ahmad Shawqi)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">poem in a similar style</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Burdat Tamim (Tamim al-Barghouti)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2010</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new poem in the Burda line; published 2013, video series 2020</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Mantle Adorned (Timothy Winter)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Tianfangshijing</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Chinese translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Commentaries by Ibn Hajar al-Haytami, Nazifi, Qastallani and others</span><span class="sg-chain-h">over 90 commentaries</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bānat Suʿād (Ka'b ibn Zuhayr)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">an earlier poem first called al-Burda (whether al-Busiri drew on it is not known)</span></li></ul></section>
